{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13728","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13728","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13728","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shubham","familyName":"Kejriwal","name":"Kejriwal, Shubham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SPLIT: Loosely-coherent inference of long-inspiral gravitational-wave sources for robustness against modeling and data outliers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T09:39:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-16T01:27:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-18T06:51:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-20T01:09:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Laser Interferometer Space Antenna (LISA) will detect gravitational waves (GWs) from dozens of extreme- and intermediate-mass-ratio inspirals (EMRIs/IMRIs). These sources will stay in-band for months to years, offering extraordinary scientific potential. However, their fully phase-coherent analysis standard in current pipelines imposes stringent waveform accuracy requirements; failing to model the signal over such long durations can result in significant systematic biases. To address this, we formulate a robust Bayesian inference framework that segments the data into blocks, infers each block coherently, and recombines the results using loosely-coherent phase information. By restricting full phase-tracking to much shorter block durations, this approach prevents significant accumulation of phase errors. We implement this methodology in \\textsc{SPLIT} (Segmented Posteriors for Long-Inspiral Templates), a GPU-accelerated Python package. Applying \\textsc{SPLIT} to an environment-rich injection, we demonstrate that while a fully-coherent vacuum-GR analysis incurs a maximum 1D systematic bias of $\\approx 4.8σ$ from the truth, the shorter integration window of our loosely-coherent approach restricts such biases to $\\lesssim 0.5σ$. Overall, despite a fractional loss of optimal signal-to-noise ratio, the substantial improvement in parameter accuracy offered by our method presents a highly advantageous trade-off for LISA and other future GW detectors."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"before proofs accepted; 28 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13728","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:39:36Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:39:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:24:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13989","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13989","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13989","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alef Iury Siqueira","familyName":"Ferreira","name":"Ferreira, Alef Iury Siqueira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucas Rafael Stefanel","familyName":"Gris","name":"Gris, Lucas Rafael Stefanel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luiz Fernando de Araújo","familyName":"Vidal","name":"Vidal, Luiz Fernando de Araújo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frederico Santos","familyName":"de Oliveira","name":"de Oliveira, Frederico Santos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher Dane","familyName":"Shulby","name":"Shulby, Christopher Dane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anderson da Silva","familyName":"Soares","name":"Soares, Anderson da Silva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arlindo Rodrigues Galvão","familyName":"Filho","name":"Filho, Arlindo Rodrigues Galvão","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mask, Sample, Revise: A Revisable CTMC Inference Stack for Guided Discrete Flow Matching Text-to-Speech"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T00:13:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:16:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-13T21:53:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-15T01:22:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent alignment-free non-autoregressive (NAR) text-to-speech (TTS) models formulate synthesis as a conditional infilling task, bypassing explicit duration predictors and external aligners. When speech is represented with neural codec tokens, the infilling problem becomes discrete, making Discrete Flow Matching (DFM), a Continuous-Time Markov Chain (CTMC) framework for discrete generation, a natural fit. However, inference-time control for stable low-step conditional infilling remains underexplored. We propose Mask, Sample, Revise, an inference-time CTMC stack for alignment-free DFM-TTS. The stack combines predictor-free guidance to strengthen text conditioning, prompt-matched conditional coupling to align the probability path with the acoustic prompt, and SC-ReMask, a schedule-constrained remasking mechanism that introduces token-to-mask transitions so de-masking decisions can be revised. These components require no post-hoc fine-tuning and operate in a single tau-leaping sampler. Controlled ablations show that this stack improves intelligibility and robustness in the low-NFE prompted setting, outperforming unguided and guidance-only samplers with substantially more steps."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13989","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:45:10Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:45:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-15T04:50:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13992","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13992","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13992","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Santiago","familyName":"Garcia","name":"Garcia, Santiago","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Group Quantization and Mellin Representations of the Heston Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mathematical Finance (q-fin.MF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Pricing of Securities (q-fin.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T00:24:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:16:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-09T05:48:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-11T00:01:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop an Affine Holonomy Group Quantization framework for the Heston stochastic volatility model. The Heston affine pricing symbol is decomposed into a finite symplectic quadratic sector and a complementary holonomy sector, leading to a Poincare-Cartan form whose characteristic flow yields the affine Riccati dynamics. Momentum polarization gives a Mellin pricing representation, while the Riccati equation admits a projective linearization. The resulting option pricing formula is validated numerically against the standard Heston solution, and the Black-Scholes model is recovered as a limiting case."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Substantially revised version. The geometric construction has been reorganized and clarified, with a sharper separation between the finite symplectic and holonomy sectors. The Cartan and characteristic structures, momentum polarization, and the projective Riccati formulation, numerical validation, and Black-Scholes reduction have been expanded"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13992","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:45:14Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:45:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-11T02:41:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13774","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13774","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13774","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jon","familyName":"Goker","name":"Goker, Jon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca V.","familyName":"Iliesiu","name":"Iliesiu, Luca V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elisa","familyName":"Tabor","name":"Tabor, Elisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A note on the quantization of angular momentum for black holes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T18:00:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:04:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-03T19:14:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-07T00:05:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We argue that the gravitational path integral for rotating black holes is periodic in the angular velocity, implying the quantization of angular momentum in arbitrary dimensions for either asymptotically flat or AdS boundary conditions. In AdS$_3$, this periodicity is a consequence of the boundary mapping class group. In higher dimensions, the periodicity arises from an infinite family of saddles labeled by integer shifts of the angular velocity unrelated to the boundary mapping class group; summing over these saddles enforces quantization independently of any large boundary diffeomorphism. We construct these saddles explicitly for Kerr-Newman black holes in both asymptotically flat space and AdS$_4$, and observe that even the path integral for the 4D Schwarzschild black hole, typically the simplest case, receives contributions from an infinite set of rotating saddles."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 Pages + 3 Appendices, 3 figures; v2: typos corrected, reference added"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13774","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:40:35Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:40:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-07T02:20:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13856","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13856","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13856","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrin","familyName":"Sam","name":"Sam, Jeffrin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dzmitry","familyName":"Tsetserukou","name":"Tsetserukou, Dzmitry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Output-Level Regularization Eliminates the Seed Lottery in Single-GPU VLA Fine-Tuning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.9; I.2.6; I.5.1","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T19:33:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:09:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-25T06:54:20Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; Some errors in the reference, and Im still developing to make it more novel"},{"date":"2026-08-26T00:34:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fine-tuning a vision-language-action model (VLA-JEPA) on a single GPU should be simple: load a pretrained checkpoint, run training, deploy. There is a hidden danger. Run the same fine-tuning code thirteen times -- same data, same architecture, different random seed -- and twelve runs produce a robot succeeding 91--94% of the time, while one run silently degrades to 65.2%: a 29 pp gap with no error message, no warning, and no way to predict which seed will fail. We call this the seed lottery. We trace the cause to output collapse: the action predictor quietly learns to produce nearly identical outputs regardless of what the robot sees. Existing weight-level methods (L2, EWC) are structurally blind to this collapse -- they penalize weight changes, but collapse occurs in directions weights can move freely without affecting outputs, a gap we formalize via the Jacobian null-space. Across 7 methods x up to 13 seeds x 3 LIBERO benchmarks, three output-level regularizers -- VICReg (n=12 seeds), Dropout (n=4), and a halved learning rate (n=5) -- each eliminate every catastrophic seed (0/21 combined collapses vs. 1/13 Baseline; F(12,11)=28.7, p\u0026lt;0.001), while weight-level methods (L2, EWC) preserve the lottery. The simplest fix is changing one number in your optimizer config."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Some errors in the reference, and Im still developing to make it more novel"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13856","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:42:20Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:42:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-26T02:42:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14229","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14229","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14229","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Adamcewicz","name":"Adamcewicz, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hugh","familyName":"McDougall","name":"McDougall, Hugh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul D.","familyName":"Lasky","name":"Lasky, Paul D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Thrane","name":"Thrane, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fortifying gravitational-wave population inference with normalizing flows"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T08:11:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:34:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-24T07:15:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T00:14:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/qq4j-1nx6","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As the LIGO-Virgo-KAGRA collaboration's (LVK's) gravitational-wave transient catalog grows, we are learning a wealth of information from the population properties of binary black hole mergers. Events in the catalog are represented with posterior samples describing the astrophysical parameters for each event. Population studies combine these samples to measure the distribution of astrophysical parameters such as black hole masses and spins. However, the posterior-sample representation of each event is only approximate. We construct a mock population with masses drawn from an astrophysically-motivated distribution with sharp features. Using this, we demonstrate that when $\\gtrsim 300$ events are combined, even with each event's posterior represented by $1 \\times 10^4 {-} 2 \\times 10^4$ samples, the numerical error can become large enough that the resulting population inference is unreliable. We consider two solutions. In the short term, we show that nested samples (already produced by LVK analyses) can be used to more accurately describe each event in population studies. But this will only grant a temporary reprieve until the nested-sample representation becomes inadequate. In the longer term, we propose to represent each event with a normalizing flow. In order to represent each event with sufficient accuracy, each normalizing flow can be used to generate an arbitrarily large number of new posterior samples with a significantly reduced computational cost relative to traditional sampling methods. When compared to nested sampling, our normalizing flows produce posterior draws with a median of $\\approx 80\\%$ fewer likelihood evaluations per sample, while also providing greater opportunity for parallelization. We believe refinement of normalizing flow architectures and training techniques in future works could further reduce this per-sample cost significantly."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages, 13 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14229","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:50:14Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:50:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T03:59:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14197","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14197","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14197","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nir","familyName":"Guttman","name":"Guttman, Nir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. Makai","familyName":"Baker","name":"Baker, A. Makai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul D.","familyName":"Lasky","name":"Lasky, Paul D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Thrane","name":"Thrane, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Licence to Bin: Accurate and Scalable Inference for Binary Neutron Stars in Next-Generation Gravitational-Wave Detectors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T07:30:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:32:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-24T00:43:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T01:10:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Next-generation gravitational-wave observatories will observe binary neutron-star mergers with much higher signal-to-noise ratios, over much longer durations and across broader frequency bands than current detectors. These long-duration signals present a major computational challenge for Bayesian parameter estimation. Reduced-order quadrature is a promising approach for accelerating inference, but in this regime its standard construction encounters severe memory and accuracy limitations. We present a practical reduced-order quadrature construction for long binary neutron-star signals with time-dependent detector response and full effects of the observatories' free-spectral range. Our approach combines improved adaptive frequency sampling, disk-backed streaming, and subbanded reduced-order quadrature construction, enabling efficient and accurate reduced-order models for signals that were previously intractable. We demonstrate for the first time reduced-order Bayesian inference on an approximately 2 h binary neutron-star signal extending down to 5 Hz and with signal-to-noise ratio 2090. We show the resulting reduced-order quadrature remains sufficiently accurate for practical inference. The full analysis is carried out in about 48 h using 128 CPU cores. We also find that, when time-dependent detector-response effects are included, a single Cosmic Explorer detector can localize such a signal to a 90% credible sky area of approximately $41~\\mathrm{deg}^2$, with important implications for multimessenger astronomy and cosmology. These results demonstrate that reduced-order methods can make next-generation binary neutron-star inference computationally feasible."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14197","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:49:33Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:49:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T03:59:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14622","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14622","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14622","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Licong","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Licong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Íñigo","familyName":"Zubeldia","name":"Zubeldia, Íñigo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Alvey","name":"Alvey, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boris","familyName":"Bolliet","name":"Bolliet, Boris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anthony","familyName":"Challinor","name":"Challinor, Anthony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Impact of non-Gaussian likelihood on cosmological constraints from the thermal Sunyaev--Zel'dovich power spectrum: a simulation-based inference analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T16:47:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-18T00:32:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/tl9p-ldwf","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The thermal Sunyaev--Zel'dovich (tSZ) power spectrum is a sensitive probe of cosmology and cluster astrophysics, but its statistics are non-Gaussian because the signal receives a significant contribution from rare, massive, low-redshift galaxy clusters. As a result, a Gaussian likelihood fails to describe the statistics of its power spectrum on large scales. We use simulation-based inference (SBI) to test the accuracy of the standard Gaussian power-spectrum likelihood for a \\textit{Planck}-like tSZ analysis. Using halo-based simulations of full-sky Compton-$y$ maps, we train neural posterior and likelihood estimators and compare the resulting constraints with those from a Gaussian likelihood assumption. Using only multipoles $\\ell \u0026lt; 1000$, we find that the Gaussian likelihood assumption gives unbiased cosmological constraints, while the SBI-based inference shows a mild broadening of the posterior distributions for the amplitudes of residual foregrounds. This suggests that the Gaussian likelihood assumption is sufficiently accurate for cosmological inference for a \\textit{Planck}-like tSZ analysis, while SBI provides a useful validation tool to model non-Gaussian likelihoods beyond analytic approximations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 15 figures, submitted to Physical Review D"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14622","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:58:33Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:58:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-18T04:05:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13842","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13842","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13842","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Hongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingtian","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Mingtian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vasileios","familyName":"Tzoumas","name":"Tzoumas, Vasileios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Domain-Adaptive Policy Learning via Kernel Representation with Application to Quadrotor Control under Non-Stationary Disturbances"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T19:16:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:08:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-24T14:43:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-27T00:43:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present an algorithm for efficient domain-adaptive policy learning via kernel representations. Learning domain-adaptive policies is challenging since it requires an environment representation that is both sufficiently expressive to model complex sim-to-real gaps during offline training, and computationally efficient enough to support rapid online adaptation during deployment. For instance, a quadrotor may encounter time-varying, non-stationary disturbances, such as sudden gusts of wind, payload shifts, or transitions between distinct flight regimes with and without ground effects. To address these challenges, we model unknown disturbances using a differentiable kernel approximation based on random Fourier features. During the offline training phase, we randomly sample kernel coefficients and bandwidth parameters to generate a rich diversity of disturbance profiles. We then optimize the control policy via differentiable simulation with analytical gradients, a process that takes only 50 seconds of training time on an RTX 4090 GPU. During hardware deployment, the policy adapts to non-stationary environments in real time by updating both the kernel coefficients and bandwidth through online least-squares estimation. We evaluate our method on quadrotor trajectory tracking tasks across high-fidelity numerical simulations and hardware experiments using Crazyflie, subjected to various disturbances, including complex aerodynamic effects, wind, ground effects, and payload fluctuations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13842","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:42:01Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:42:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-27T01:55:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14567","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14567","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14567","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rameez","familyName":"Iqbal","name":"Iqbal, Rameez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gautier","familyName":"Rouaze","name":"Rouaze, Gautier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gio","familyName":"Bellone","name":"Bellone, Gio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Minola","name":"Minola, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lenan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lenan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengmao","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Zhengmao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scale-Separated Collective Bubble Nucleation and Departure"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T15:44:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:56:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-09T09:01:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-10T00:29:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Nucleation is classically treated as a local process, yet whether coupling between neighboring sites governs activation and stability remains unexplored. Here we show bubble nucleation is fundamentally collective: sites separated by the hydrodynamic-boundary-layer scale activate more readily and resist deactivation under changing thermal loads, consistent with a non-local hydrodynamic shielding mechanism, whereby neighboring bubbles slow the intervening flow, suppress convective heat removal, and stabilize vapor embryos. Using surfaces with two independently tunable length scales, we isolate this near-wall coupling from a second collective process, coalescence between departing bubble clusters, which transitions through isolated, promotive, and excessive regimes as the departure diameter grows with heat flux. The dominant length scale thus shifts with operating conditions, from boundary-layer coupling near activation to departure-scale coupling once nucleation is established. These results establish a scale-dependent framework for collective nucleation and departure, broadly related to phase change processes on structured surfaces."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14567","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:57:25Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:57:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-10T01:59:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14603","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14603","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14603","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefano","familyName":"Maestri","name":"Maestri, Stefano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards In Silico Cancer Therapy Design: An Agent-Based Approach for GPU-Accelerated Molecular Pathway Simulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Molecular Networks (q-bio.MN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.6.3; J.3","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T16:25:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:58:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agent-based modelling is gaining recognition as a powerful approach for simulating complex cellular pathways, owing to its ability to reproduce emergent biological behaviours without requiring extensive kinetic parameterisation. In this article, we present a GPU-accelerated agent-based simulator specifically designed to model and analyse signalling pathways involved in cancer progression, and to evaluate therapeutic interventions. Our approach leverages the computing capabilities of FLAME GPU 2, a GPU-accelerated agent-based modelling framework, to efficiently manage simulations involving millions of molecules interacting within a three-dimensional environment. Each molecule is represented as an autonomous agent with defined physical properties, capable of binding, releasing reaction products, migrating between compartments, and interacting based on spatial proximity. An intuitive graphical interface supports model construction, parameter setup, and real-time modification of treatment strategies. As the primary focus of this paper, we validate the simulator on the MAPK/ERK cascade affected by the BRAFV600E mutation, demonstrating that it accurately reproduces dose-response trends observed in clinical data and outperforms both deterministic models and our prior agent-based implementations. A second case study extends the approach to nuclear signalling by reproducing the dynamics of cFos expression and phosphorylation. This demonstrates the simulator's ability to capture compartmentalised regulation, reproducing transient mRNA responses and protein accumulation, including the effect of an unresolved negative transcriptional regulator. Together, these results show that GPU-accelerated ABM can faithfully replicate both drug response and emergent gene expression dynamics, providing a scalable and biologically grounded computational tool for supporting precision oncology."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 7 figures, 2 tables. A preliminary version of this work appeared in the Collections of Short Papers of CIBB 2025 (20th International Conference on Computational Intelligence Methods for Bioinformatics and Biostatistics, Milan, 10-12 September 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14603","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:58:10Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:58:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:58:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14598","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14598","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14598","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Asaria","name":"Asaria, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tony","familyName":"Salomone","name":"Salomone, Tony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deep","familyName":"Gandhi","name":"Gandhi, Deep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Realizing Native INT8 Compute for Diffusion Transformers on Consumer GPUs: A Fused INT8 GEMM Kernel for Ideogram 4.0"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T16:19:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:57:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Post-training INT8 (W8A8) quantization of diffusion transformers is widely deployed as a speed optimization, yet on consumer Ampere GPUs it is frequently slower than the FP8 and NF4 alternatives it is meant to beat. We trace this to a software artifact: the production \"INT8\" forward quantizes weights and activations only to immediately dequantize them back to bf16 and run a bf16 matrix multiply, never engaging the GPU's INT8 tensor cores, so the hardware's compute advantage is left entirely unrealized. We close this gap with a single fused Triton INT8 GEMM (int8xint8-\u0026gt;int32 on Ampere tensor cores, with per-token x per-channel dequantization and bias folded into the epilogue, autotuned per GEMM shape) dropped into the Ideogram 4.0 diffusion transformer's linear layers in place of the dequantize-to-bf16 path. In the kernel, the int8xint8-\u0026gt;int32 accumulation is bit-exact against torch._int_mm and the dequantized output matches the reference at cosine similarity 1.0 with no NaNs, running 2.8-4.2x faster than bf16 per GEMM. End to end it delivers a ~1.1x (~9-10%) speedup at 768px, and at 1024px it generates an image in 156.5 s on a single RTX 3090, faster than the single-card NF4 (164.5 s) and FP8 (172.9 s) baselines, at no measurable quality cost on these point estimates (PickScore/CLIPScore). INT8 thus goes from the slowest variant to the fastest, and 1024px becomes single-GPU feasible. The primary speed criterion (beat FP8, by ~9.5%) is comfortably met; the NF4 margin (~4.9%, single-run n=4) is within run-to-run variance we did not quantify and is best read as consistent with meeting the stretch target. We close with an honest deployment map: the win is specific to consumer Ampere, and on A100 and B200 the same kernel loses to those cards' fast native bf16/FP8 paths."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14598","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:58:04Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:58:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:58:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14514","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14514","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14514","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adriana Laurindo","familyName":"Monteiro","name":"Monteiro, Adriana Laurindo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roberto Imbuzeiro","familyName":"Oliveira","name":"Oliveira, Roberto Imbuzeiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Nonparametric inference on Fokker-Plank and McKean-Vlasov models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T14:45:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:52:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a kernel-based estimator of the velocity field governing the transport and diffusion of $d$-dimensional interacting particles. Assuming the initial positions are i.i.d. with law $μ_0$, we establish consistency of the estimator with an explicit mean-squared error rate of order $h^2 + N^(-2/(d+2))$, where $h$ denotes the time-discretization step and N the number of particles. The analysis covers two distinct settings: the Fokker-Planck equation, where we recover the underlying potential function, and the McKean-Vlasov equation, where we deconvolve the interaction kernel driving the mean-field dynamics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14514","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:56:18Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:56:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:56:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14493","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14493","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14493","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying-Han","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Ying-Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bas","familyName":"Dorsman","name":"Dorsman, Bas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna L.","familyName":"Watts","name":"Watts, Anna L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuomo","familyName":"Salmi","name":"Salmi, Tuomo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juri","familyName":"Poutanen","name":"Poutanen, Juri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang-Dong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiang-Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pulse profile modelling of accreting millisecond pulsars with disc occultation and its impact on parameter inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T14:29:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:51:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pulse profile modelling is a relativistic ray-tracing technique used to infer neutron star mass, radius, and surface hotspot properties from X-ray pulsations. Pulse profile modelling has been widely applied to rotation-powered millisecond pulsars, where the local environment is relatively empty. Application to accreting millisecond pulsars is complicated by the geometry of the local accretion flow, including disc occultation of surface emission. In this work, we extend an established pulse profile modelling code, X-PSI, to incorporate accretion disc occultation in accreting millisecond pulsar pulse profile modelling. We quantify how disc occultation depends on system geometry and evaluate its impact on parameter inference. We find that disc occultation is primarily governed by the viewing inclination and can significantly reshape pulse profiles at moderate to high inclinations. Using synthetic Neutron Star Interior Composition Explorer datasets, we investigate parameter recovery for two representative hotspot configurations. For hotspots close to the rotational poles, statistically acceptable fits can yield posteriors that deviate noticeably from the true parameters. In contrast, in a case with hotspots located closer to the equator we find more reliable parameter recovery. We further find that neglecting disc occultation can introduce spurious posterior modes with comparable statistical support, potentially affecting the interpretation of inferred neutron star parameters, suggesting that this effect should be included in accreting millisecond pulsar pulse profile modelling."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 9 figures, 4 tables. Submitted to MNRAS"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14493","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:55:52Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:55:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:55:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14480","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14480","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14480","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christina","familyName":"Agapopoulou","name":"Agapopoulou, Christina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas","familyName":"Calefice","name":"Calefice, Lukas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Álvaro Fernández","familyName":"Casani","name":"Casani, Álvaro Fernández","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vava","familyName":"Gligorov","name":"Gligorov, Vava","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arthur Marius","familyName":"Hennequin","name":"Hennequin, Arthur Marius","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Louis","familyName":"Henry","name":"Henry, Louis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valerii","familyName":"Kholoimov","name":"Kholoimov, Valerii","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brij Kishor","familyName":"Jashal","name":"Jashal, Brij Kishor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arantza","familyName":"Oyanguren","name":"Oyanguren, Arantza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Pica","name":"Pica, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Volodymyr","familyName":"Svintozelskyi","name":"Svintozelskyi, Volodymyr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Da Yu","familyName":"Tou","name":"Tou, Da Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahui","familyName":"Zhuo","name":"Zhuo, Jiahui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Seeding and Matching algorithms for the first GPU-based High Level Trigger of the LHCb experiment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Detectors (physics.ins-det)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Experiment (hep-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T14:18:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:51:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We describe the GPU implementation of the Seeding and Matching algorithms, developed for the first level trigger of the LHCb experiment and key to reconstruct long and very displaced tracks at 40 MHz. The algorithms have been participating in the data taking during the full Run 3 of LHCb with a very high throughput, increasing the physics reach of the experiment.\n The Seeding is a standalone pattern recognition algorithm aiming at finding charged particle trajectories in the most forward tracker of LHCb. These trajectories are then extrapolated backward by the Matching algorithm which combines them with stubs formed from hits in the first tracker in order to form what we call Long tracks. Hits in the second tracker are then searched for to better define the trajectory and improve the track momentum resolution. This backward approach, complementary to the approach of extrapolating the stubs in the first detector to the forward tracker through the magnetic field, improves the Long track efficiency at low transverse momenta, increasing the potential of key physics decay channels."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To be submitted to Computing and Software for Big Science"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14480","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:55:34Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:55:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:55:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14446","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14446","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14446","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debora","familyName":"Amadori","name":"Amadori, Debora","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cleopatra","familyName":"Christoforou","name":"Christoforou, Cleopatra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gianmarco","familyName":"Cipollone","name":"Cipollone, Gianmarco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Decay of periodic entropy solutions to Euler-alignment systems with non-constant kernel"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T13:29:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:49:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider a hydrodynamic model of flocking-type with pressure on the torus, with integrable interaction kernel and density bounded away from zero. We prove that, if an entropy weak solution exists, then its $L^2$ norm decays exponentially fast in time towards the mean values on the period. The proof relies on the study of a suitable energy functional that combines a strictly convex entropy for the system and a potential term, and this allows us to treat the nonlocal source term for a class of strictly positive convolution kernels in $L^1$."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14446","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:54:51Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:54:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:54:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14194","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14194","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14194","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tia","familyName":"Tiwari","name":"Tiwari, Tia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vamshi Krishna","familyName":"Kancharla","name":"Kancharla, Vamshi Krishna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neelam","familyName":"Sinha","name":"Sinha, Neelam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hybrid Classical-Quantum (HCQ) Alzheimer's Classification via Supervised $β$-VAE and Quantum Kernels"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T07:27:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:32:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents a two-stage Hybrid Classical-Quantum (HCQ) pipeline for binary Alzheimer's disease (AD) classification from 3D T1-weighted structural MRI volumes, where the classical and quantum components are designed to complement each other rather than operate independently. A supervised 3D $β$-variational autoencoder (VAE) is trained end-to-end under voxel-wise reconstruction, KL-divergence, and focal classification losses that compress each 3D MRI volume (resized from 152 x 184 x 152 to 96 x 96 x 96) into a 64-dimensional latent code. Partial Least Squares (PLS) regression selects the six components in the latent code that best separate Alzheimer's Disease (AD) from cognitively normal (CN) subjects and rescales them into rotation angles, which are encoded onto a six-qubit register using the ZZ quantum feature map to give us the respective quantum states. The input to a precomputed-kernel Support Vector Machine (SVM) is an N x N Gram matrix (N = 308), created by calculating the overlap between every pair of quantum states. The novelty of this work lies in the fact that the quantum kernel operates directly on disease-aware features that are learned end-to-end by a supervised autoencoder, rather than on pre-extracted inputs. On 308 ADNI-1 subjects, consisting of 137 AD and 171 CN subjects, the baseline achieved 67.2% accuracy and 0.759 AUC, while the stability-enhanced variant reached 72.1% accuracy and 0.799 AUC with cross-fold variance halved. 3D Grad-CAM further helped validate our model's focus on brain regions linked to Alzheimer's. The HCQ pipeline could serve as a general-purpose framework for diagnostic classification across biomedical imaging domains that present similar challenges for classical approaches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14194","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:49:29Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:49:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:49:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14132","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14132","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14132","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingying","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Yingying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lan","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Lan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daoji","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Daoji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinchi","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Jinchi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HSCI: Neyman-Orthogonal Causal Inference under High-Dimensional Proportional Hazards"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T05:39:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:28:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Valid treatment effect inference in survival studies is fundamental yet challenging when the treatment assignments and outcomes are confounded by many baseline covariates. To this end, in this paper we propose a high-dimensional survival causal inference (HSCI) framework that delivers valid inference under a sparse high-dimensional Cox proportional hazards outcome model and a high-dimensional logistic propensity score working model. To mitigate the nuisance estimation bias, we develop a Neyman near-orthogonal score for the treatment effect and implement it with cross-fitting. Under doubly robust nuisance-rate conditions, we establish the root-n asymptotic normality and consistent variance estimation. We also extend the framework to inference on high-dimensional survival covariate effects. Simulation examples confirm that HSCI reduces sharply the bias relative to the regularized Cox estimators and maintains valid confidence interval coverage across different dimensionality, censoring, and misspecified propensity-model settings. An application to diffuse large-B-cell lymphoma data further showcases its value for high-dimensional biomedical survival studies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14132","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:48:12Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:48:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:48:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14085","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14085","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14085","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haodi","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Haodi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Natalia","familyName":"Nolde","name":"Nolde, Natalia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bias-corrected empirical likelihood-based inference for the tail index under heavy-tailed models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"62G20, 62G32, 62E20","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T04:00:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:24:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The tail index parameter of heavy-tailed probability models plays a key role in characterizing the tail decay of the underlying distribution function and is often involved in extrapolation procedures for various extreme value analysis questions. In this paper we revisit the question of tail index estimation and combine the ideas of bias-correction and empirical likelihood estimation to propose an estimator that offers an attractive alternative to some of the existing estimators. We develop an asymptotic theory for the proposed estimator and conduct simulation studies to demonstrate its performance in finite sample situations. The method is also applied to a data example for illustration."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14085","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:47:12Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:47:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:47:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14057","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14057","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14057","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyong","familyName":"Hahn","name":"Hahn, Jinyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niu","familyName":"He","name":"He, Niu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhipeng","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Zhipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenyu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Granular Instrumental Variables: Estimation and Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Econometrics (econ.EM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T03:09:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:22:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop an estimation and inference framework for granular instrumental variables (GIVs) in models with latent aggregate shocks. Our key insight is that valid GIVs are characterized by the orthogonal complement of the factor-loading space. This characterization yields a feasible procedure for constructing GIVs when factor loadings are unknown and does not require a large cross-sectional dimension. We provide practical procedures for inference and specification testing, and apply the framework to estimate the aggregate equity market multiplier. Our empirical results reveal substantial heterogeneity in equity demand elasticities across investor sectors and may provide nuanced support for the inelastic-markets hypothesis."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14057","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:46:37Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:46:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:46:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14030","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14030","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14030","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishit","familyName":"Chatterjee","name":"Chatterjee, Rishit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tahiya","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, Tahiya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficiency-Performance Trade-offs in Neural Speaker Diarization via Structured Pruning and Low-Bit Quantization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T02:10:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:19:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Streaming speaker diarization is crucial for time-critical medical dispatch, but deploying it on resource-constrained hardware requires smaller, faster models. Using SIMSAMU, a dataset of simulated medical-dispatch conversations, we evaluate streaming behavior before compressing the segmentation model with pruning and low-bit quantization. We characterize performance across a range of streaming latency budgets and find that additional buffering is not consistently beneficial, while very low-latency operating points can substantially degrade performance. Our study shows that model compression trades performance for memory footprint, and we highlight an operating point where FP16 reduces model size by half with essentially unchanged real-time factor, at a cost of a 40\\% relative DER increase against the baseline. This work characterizes the trade-offs for real-time deployment and contributes to speech technology that can enable reliable human communication in time-critical contexts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 3 figures, preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14030","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:46:04Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:46:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:46:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14007","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14007","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14007","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel Taiwo","familyName":"Fatunmbi","name":"Fatunmbi, Samuel Taiwo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Om Amit","familyName":"Gandhi","name":"Gandhi, Om Amit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luke","familyName":"Logan","name":"Logan, Luke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VaultxGPU: GPU-Accelerated Blockchain Consensus"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T00:58:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:17:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Blockchain consensus mechanisms based on Proof-of-Work consume significant energy, with Bitcoin alone estimated at approximately 150 TWh per year. Proof-of-Space reduces this cost by replacing repeated computation with storage, but plot generation remains bottlenecked by CPU hashing throughput. Prior work on VaultX demonstrated a high-performance CPU-based Proof-of-Space plotter using multi-threaded Blake3 hashing, achieving plotting speeds 4 to 50x faster than Chia depending on hardware configuration. In this paper, we present VaultxGPU, a GPU-accelerated extension of the VaultX plotter that offloads the Blake3 hashing pipeline to the GPU using custom kernels. We implement the plotter in both CUDA for NVIDIA hardware and SYCL for AMD and Intel GPUs, keeping Table 1 entirely in GPU VRAM and fusing the sort and match stages into a single kernel to minimize data movement. We evaluate VaultxGPU across K-values 27 through 31 against CPU baselines. Our SYCL GPU implementation achieves a 59.2x speedup over a single-threaded CPU baseline, completing a K=31 plot in 45.4 seconds compared to 2688 seconds, and outperforms even the best 384-thread CPU configuration. These results confirm that GPU acceleration is the correct direction for scaling Proof-of-Space plotting beyond what CPU parallelism can achieve."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14007","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:45:33Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:45:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:45:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14000","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.14000","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.14000","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Theodore","familyName":"Meek","name":"Meek, Theodore","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Di Qiu","familyName":"Xiang","name":"Xiang, Di Qiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Chess","name":"Chess, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vasily","familyName":"Ilin","name":"Ilin, Vasily","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Formalizing Numerical Analysis: An Agent Pipeline and Quality Audit Beyond Kernel Acceptance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-12T00:45:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:17:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent work has demonstrated that coding agents can formalize entire advanced mathematics textbooks in Lean 4, yet existing efforts concentrate on branches of mathematics already well-represented in mathlib and measure success solely through kernel acceptance. We address both limitations by applying a coding agent to formalize Numerical Methods for Ordinary Differential Equations, a textbook in numerical analysis that is largely absent from mathlib, stressing the agent's capacity to develop new theory from scratch. We further introduce a systematic, reproducible three-dimensional framework for evaluating the quality of agent-produced formalizations beyond compilation: semantic correctness, Mathlib reuse, and cross-file reuse via LLM-as-judge methods. Applying this framework to our own formalization and to the released outputs of RepoProver and M2F, we uncover recurring unfaithful formalization patterns, including incomplete multi-part statements, added weakening hypotheses, and parameter restrictions, that kernel acceptance entirely obscures. Our results suggest that compilation-based metrics substantially overstate formalization quality, and we provide a reproducible audit methodology to support more rigorous evaluation of future autoformalization systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.14000","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:45:24Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:45:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:45:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13968","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13968","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13968","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anas","familyName":"Nassar","name":"Nassar, Anas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steve","familyName":"Mohr","name":"Mohr, Steve","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leonard","familyName":"Apanasevich","name":"Apanasevich, Leonard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Himanshu","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Himanshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"STREAM: Multi-Tier LLM Inference Middleware with Dual-Channel HPC Token Streaming"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T23:20:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:15:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Researchers and practitioners working with large language models face a fragmented landscape: local models are free and private but hardware limits the model size and context windows a researcher can use; institutional HPC centers offer powerful GPU resources at no marginal cost and keep data within institutional boundaries, but operate behind firewalls and are designed for batch jobs rather than interactive use; commercial cloud APIs provide frontier-model quality on demand but impose significant cost and data retention policies unsuitable for sensitive research data. No existing system unifies all three. STREAM (Smart Tiered Routing Engine for AI Models) addresses this gap with four contributions: (1) a three-tier routing architecture combining local, HPC, and cloud inference with a local LLM-based complexity judge; (2) a dual-channel HPC streaming architecture that separates the Globus Compute control plane (authentication and job dispatch) from a WebSocket relay data plane (token delivery), enabling sub-second TTFT (0.54 s median, 21.1x over batch mode's 11.40 s) through institutional firewalls without VPN or firewall rule changes, with end-to-end AES-256-GCM encryption ensuring the relay operator cannot read token payloads; (3) tier-aware context summarization that prevents long conversations from forcing simple queries onto expensive tiers; and (4) an HPC-as-API proxy mode that exposes HPC inference as an OpenAI-compatible endpoint callable from any standard client with no HPC expertise, a deployment pattern made practical only by the sub-second TTFT of contribution (2). Llama 3.2 3B achieves 85.1% free-tier retention on a 1,200-query benchmark spanning ten domains. Measured TTFT: 0.26 s local, 0.54 s HPC (relay), 1.68 s cloud."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 1 figure, PEARC '26"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13968","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:44:44Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:44:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:44:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13932","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13932","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13932","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M. V.","familyName":"Takook","name":"Takook, M. V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Krein Space Quantization and a Spectral Interpretation of the Riemann $ξ$-Function"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T21:51:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:13:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1142/s0217732326500756","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The invariant two-point function of a scalar field in de Sitter spacetime can be expressed in terms of Legendre functions via Lorentzian harmonic analysis. Using this structure together with the Mehler--Fock transform, we obtain an integral representation of the completed Riemann $ξ$-function in which the Legendre kernel appears naturally. Motivated by this correspondence, we introduce a retarded propagator whose spectral weight is given by the $ξ$-function and analyze it within the framework of Krein space quantization, where sign-indefinite spectral measures are admissible. This construction yields a geometric and spectral interpretation of the $ξ$-function restricted to the critical line and relates the asymptotic spacing of its zeros to a mass--time scaling in de Sitter geometry. The results provide a novel interpretive framework linking de Sitter quantum field theory, harmonic analysis, and analytic number theory."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13932","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2026-06-15T01:43:58Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:43:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:43:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13864","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13864","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13864","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ilya","familyName":"Archakov","name":"Archakov, Ilya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter Reinhard","familyName":"Hansen","name":"Hansen, Peter Reinhard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Generalized Fisher Transformation: Finite-Sample Properties and Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Econometrics (econ.EM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T19:44:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:09:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the finite-sample behavior of the Generalized Fisher Transformation (GFT), the parametrization of a correlation matrix $C$ by $γ(C)=\\operatorname{vecl}\\log C$. The GFT coordinates extend Fisher's transformation to dimension $n\u0026gt;2$: for elliptical data their finite-sample distributions are close to Gaussian. More strikingly, the coordinates are nearly uncorrelated and their covariance is largely invariant to $C$. This approximate orthogonality and invariance make GFT-based inference far better behaved in finite samples than inference based on sample correlations or element-wise Fisher transformed correlations, yielding estimation errors that are approximately Gaussian, weakly dependent, and nearly pivotal."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13864","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:42:32Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:42:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:42:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13850","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13850","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13850","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lekha","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Lekha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis","familyName":"Damiano","name":"Damiano, Luis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Controller-Augmented Hidden Markov Models: A Computational Framework for Constrained Sequential Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T19:29:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:09:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hidden Markov models are foundational for sequential inference, but their Markovian assumption fails under pathwise constraints such as precedence requirements, visitation cardinalities, or monotonic state progression, which induce long-range dependencies that invalidate standard dynamic programming algorithms. To deal with this, we present Controller-Augmented Hidden Markov Models (CHMMs), a framework that compiles each constraint into a finite-state controller tracking the minimal sufficient history, after which standard forward--backward and Viterbi recursions on the augmented chain compute exact constrained posteriors and maximum a posteriori paths in both discrete and continuous time, the latter through uniformization. We establish four theoretical guarantees: exactness of constrained inference, monotone ascent of constrained EM, inference complexity linear in the controller cardinality, and a total-variation bound under constraint misspecification. A catalog of controller encodings covering 11 constraint families across the ordering, visitation, path, and temporal categories operationalizes the framework. Empirically, we evaluate CHMMs against 6 alternative decoders on 3 real-world sequence-labeling tasks of substantively different character: gene-structure decoding in \\emph{Drosophila melanogaster}, free-living activity recognition in CASAS smart-home environments, and protocol-defined human activity recognition from wearable sensors. The results reveal a clean local-versus-cumulative dichotomy in which controller augmentation is uniquely able to recover globally feasible trajectories on cumulative-constraint regimes, whilst simpler decoders are matched in validity on locally-dominated regimes. Together, theory and experiment characterize when exact controller augmentation is necessary and when simpler approaches suffice."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13850","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:42:12Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:42:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:42:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13830","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13830","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13830","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomas","familyName":"Aguiar","name":"Aguiar, Tomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcos Cesar","familyName":"de Oliveira","name":"de Oliveira, Marcos Cesar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Collective-Spin Derivation of the Uniform Magnon Hamiltonian in Cavity Magnonics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T19:01:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:08:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a direct collective-spin derivation of the effective uniform-mode Hamiltonian used in cavity magnonics. Starting from a nearest-neighbor Heisenberg ferromagnet coupled to long-wavelength magnetic fields, we show that the relevant dynamics can be restricted to the fully symmetric spin sector, where the exchange interaction contributes only a constant energy shift and the ferromagnet behaves as a macrospin of length $Ns$. Applying the Holstein--Primakoff transformation directly to this total spin yields the usual uniform magnon mode and its leading nonlinear corrections without first introducing site-resolved bosonic operators. This collective formulation makes explicit the interpretation of the ferromagnet as a synthetic large-spin atom and provides a compact route to the effective Hamiltonians used in driven and Floquet cavity magnonics. As a physical consequence, the leading nonlinear correction produces an occupation-dependent reduction of the effective magnon--photon coupling, providing a simple signature of finite-spin saturation under strong uniform-mode driving."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13830","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:41:46Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:41:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:41:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13742","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13742","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13742","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian","familyName":"Paischer","name":"Paischer, Fabian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dylan","familyName":"Rubini","name":"Rubini, Dylan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deniz A.","familyName":"Bezgin","name":"Bezgin, Deniz A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron B.","familyName":"Buhendwa","name":"Buhendwa, Aaron B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Hauser","name":"Hauser, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florian","familyName":"Sestak","name":"Sestak, Florian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johannes","familyName":"Brandstetter","name":"Brandstetter, Johannes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Kaltenbach","name":"Kaltenbach, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolaus A.","familyName":"Adams","name":"Adams, Nikolaus A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A fully GPU-based workflow for building physics emulators of hypersonic flows"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Fluid Dynamics (physics.flu-dyn)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T13:11:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:03:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The ability to resolve complex physical phenomena with high fidelity and at low computational cost is central to addressing key challenges in modern engineering. A prime example lies in hypersonic flows, where the precise prediction of the full flowfield topology, in particular with respect to shock wave location and intensity, is critical. Yet supersonic and hypersonic flows continue to be a stumbling block for traditional reduced-order models and neural emulators that struggle to capture steep gradients in flow states with physical consistency in applications of industrial relevance. To that end, we introduce a fully GPU based workflow that integrates accelerated data generation with the training of neural emulators augmented by uncertainty quantification and physics-aware refinement. Our workflow is enabled by a differentiable high-fidelity solver (JAX-Fluids) which we employ for rapid dataset creation and residual-based improvement of the neural emulator to enhance physical consistency. Building on this framework, we first present a suite of model architectures and analyze their scaling behavior to expose their strengths and shortcomings. We then show that residual-based refinement enables training on cases where only mesh and input parameters are available, substantially reducing residuals and improving physical consistency. Together, differentiable simulation and residual-based refinement yield physics emulators that remain reliable beyond their training distribution, a key requirement for deploying surrogates in real-world engineering design loops."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"First authors contributed equally"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13742","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:39:53Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:39:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:39:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13740","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13740","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13740","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuowei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Tuowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanfan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yanfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ju","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Ju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient On-Device Diffusion LLM Inference with Mobile NPU"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-06-11T12:44:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:02:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion large language models (dLLMs) accelerate generation by denoising multiple tokens in parallel, making them attractive for latency-sensitive mobile inference. However, repeated denoising introduces substantial computation on smartphones. Mobile neural processing units (NPUs) offer high-throughput dense matrix computation, but efficiently exploiting them remains challenging: token commitment shrinks per-block effective workloads, token revision complicates KV cache reuse, and limited NPU-visible address space incurs costly remapping and data transfer overheads.\n In this paper, we propose llada.cpp, the first NPU-aware inference framework for accelerating dLLMs on smartphones. llada.cpp aligns block-wise dLLM inference with the execution characteristics of mobile NPUs through three techniques. (1) Multi-Block Speculative Decoding fills the shrinking workload in late-stage current-block decoding with speculative future-block tokens. (2) Dual-Path Progressive Revision keeps committed tokens revisable until stable and refreshes unstable tokens through a CPU-side path without stalling dense NPU execution. (3) Swap-Optimized Memory Runtime compacts NPU-visible address layouts and overlaps data staging with NPU computation to reduce remapping and transfer overheads. We implement llada.cpp as an end-to-end framework and evaluate it across diverse hardware platforms and dLLM workloads. llada.cpp reduces LLaDA-8B generation latency by 17x-42x over the CPU baseline with prefix KV cache reuse, while preserving generation quality."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13740","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:39:50Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:39:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:39:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13698","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2606.13698","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2606.13698","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dénes","familyName":"Toth","name":"Toth, Dénes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"George","familyName":"Ambroladze","name":"Ambroladze, George","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edwin","familyName":"Sundberg","name":"Sundberg, Edwin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Beikmohammadi","name":"Beikmohammadi, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alfreds","familyName":"Lapkovskis","name":"Lapkovskis, Alfreds","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Active Inference for Adaptive Traffic Signal Control in Noisy Nonstationary IoT Environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2026,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2026-05-31T19:15:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-15T00:00:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Urban traffic signal control at IoT-instrumented intersections must remain effective under sensor occlusion, weather attenuation, and nonstationary demand. Conventional controllers degrade under these conditions, and learned policies remain difficult to audit. To address these challenges, we propose an active inference controller for a four-arm signalized intersection that dynamically selects phases by minimizing expected free energy (EFE) over Gaussian beliefs about per-direction congestion levels, yielding a fully traceable decision pipeline. We benchmark the controller in a SUMO traffic simulator against a rule-based heuristic and a deep Q-network (DQN) across four scenarios that progressively increase noise and nonstationarity, spanning sensor occlusion, adverse weather, and stochastic accidents. Across 100 independent random evaluations per scenario, active inference attains the lowest idle times and CO2 emissions in the noisiest scenarios (56,977 s and 29.12 kg vs. 71,741 s and 30.56 kg for DQN). These gains come at a modest cost in bus priority service rate and phase switch frequency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to IEEE 12th World Forum on Internet of Things (WF-IoT) 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.13698","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2026-06-15T01:38:59Z","registered":"2026-06-15T01:38:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-15T01:38:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":31,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2026-06-14T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2026-06-15T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28GPU+OR+GPUs+OR+kernel+OR+kernels+OR+collective+OR+inference+OR+serving+OR+quantization+OR+%22distributed+training%22+OR+%22tensor+parallel%22+OR+%22pipeline+parallel%22+OR+%22KV+cache%22%29%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=1000"}}